 # Hegar Dilators
 
-**Blunt-tipped, cylindrical, graduated metal dilators sized in millimeters (1–26 mm)** — the metric-sized workhorse for **mechanical cervical dilation** in gynecologic and obstetric procedures, and a recurring tool in urogyn / reconstructive-urology practice for **postmenopausal labial-fusion separation**, **cervical dilation during concomitant hysterectomy**, **urethral calibration**, and **catheterizable-stoma assessment**. Named for **Ernst Ludwig Alfred Hegar (1830–1914)**, the German gynecologist who also lends his name to the [Mayo-Hegar](/docs/foundations/tools/instruments/needle-holders/mayo-hegar) and [Olsen-Hegar](/docs/foundations/tools/instruments/needle-holders/olsen-hegar) needle holders.<sup>[[1]](#ref1)</sup>
+Hegar dilators are reusable or sterile single-use **metric cervical dilators**. Current products vary: they may be single- or double-ended, straight or slightly curved, and offered in whole- or half-millimeter increments. The Hegar number generally denotes diameter in millimeters, but the available range and paired ends depend on the manufacturer.<sup>[[1]](#ref1)[[2]](#ref2)</sup>
 
-## Design
+## Current Configurations
 
-- **Blunt, rounded tip** — distinct from the tapered Pratt; the blunt nose lets the operator feel the **loss-of-resistance pop at the internal cervical os** (the principal tactile cue during cervical dilation).<sup>[[1]](#ref1)</sup>
-- **Cylindrical shaft** with uniform working-length diameter; sizes step in **1 mm or 0.5 mm increments**.
-- **Double-ended** — one instrument carries two sizes; the standard set spans roughly 1–26 mm.
-- **Stainless steel**, autoclavable.
-- **Faster dilation per pass** than tapered dilators because each step advances the diameter by a full millimeter; the trade-off is that the blunt geometry demands **more axial force** than a tapered Pratt.<sup>[[1]](#ref1)</sup>
+- CooperSurgical lists a stainless-steel double-ended set of eight spanning 3/4 through 17/18 mm.<sup>[[1]](#ref1)</sup>
+- The STERIS catalog includes single-ended whole- and half-millimeter sizes and double-ended sets extending to 25/26 mm.<sup>[[2]](#ref2)</sup>
+- Other current systems differ in length, curvature, tip taper, sterility, and packaging. “Hegar 8” conveys diameter, not every other device property.
 
-## Sizing — Hegar (mm) vs Pratt (Fr)
+For conversion, **3 French = 1 mm**. Conversion does not make a cervical dilator automatically compatible with a urethral, stomal, or neovaginal task.
 
-Hegar dilators are sized in **millimeters of diameter**; Pratt dilators (and most urology sounds) use the **French system (1 Fr = 0.33 mm)**.
+## Principal Use: Cervical Dilation
 
-| Hegar (mm) | Approx. French | Typical use |
-|---|---|---|
-| 4 | 12 Fr | Sound-only access; early diagnostic hysteroscopy |
-| 6 | 18 Fr | Diagnostic hysteroscope insertion |
-| 8 | 24 Fr | Operative hysteroscope |
-| 9 | 27 Fr | Operative hysteroscope (resect-ready) |
-| 10–11 | 30–33 Fr | Resectoscope insertion |
+Hegar dilators are passed sequentially to enlarge the cervical canal for hysteroscopy, uterine evacuation, sampling, drainage, or another defined gynecologic procedure. The necessary endpoint is the diameter required by the selected scope or instrument—not a universal table tied to procedure names.
 
-A preoperative cervical width of **≥ 5 mm** is the standard "satisfactory" threshold — it permits diagnostic hysteroscope insertion without further dilation and reduces the risk of false-passage creation on subsequent dilation.<sup>[[2]](#ref2)</sup>
+Safe technique depends on knowing uterine position and cervical anatomy, stabilizing the cervix when appropriate, using adequate analgesia/anesthesia, and advancing in small increments without force. A subjective “pop” or loss of resistance must not be treated as proof that the dilator remains in the uterine cavity; unexpected depth, direction, pain, or resistance requires reassessment.
 
-## Reconstructive-Urology and Urogyn Uses
+### Cervical preparation
 
-### Concomitant gynecologic dilation during urogyn surgery
+A Cochrane review found that cervical-ripening agents before operative hysteroscopy can increase baseline dilation and reduce the need for mechanical dilation, but trials used different agents, routes, doses, intervals, populations, and procedures. A regimen such as “400 µg vaginal misoprostol 12 hours before every case” is therefore not a universal Hegar instruction. Use current procedure- and patient-specific guidance, including pregnancy status and contraindications.<sup>[[3]](#ref3)</sup>
 
-- **Cervical dilation before hysteroscopy** as part of the workup for postmenopausal bleeding, mesh-erosion–related symptoms, or pre-prolapse-surgery endometrial assessment. Standard target: Hegar **No. 6** diagnostic, **No. 8–9** operative, **No. 10–11** resectoscope.<sup>[[2]](#ref2)</sup>
-- **Cervical dilation at the time of vaginal or abdominal hysterectomy** when concurrent endometrial sampling or hysteroscopic evaluation is planned, and for postoperative lochia / hematometra drainage in selected cesarean cases (Cochrane: low-quality evidence; unclear PPH / transfusion / endometritis benefit).<sup>[[3]](#ref3)</sup>
+The Society of Family Planning review of first-trimester surgical abortion describes several mechanical and pharmacologic preparation strategies and the uncommon but serious risks of cervical laceration and uterine perforation. It does not establish one Hegar design or size sequence as universally preferred.<sup>[[7]](#ref7)</sup>
 
-### Postmenopausal labial-fusion separation
+## Selected Reconstructive/Urogyn Uses
 
-- **Nonsurgical Hegar-dilator separation of complete labial fusion** in postmenopausal patients when topical estrogen has failed. Serial Hegar dilators are used for **blunt dissection** to restore introital anatomy without sharp incision — a urogyn-specific use case described by Kaplan and colleagues.<sup>[[8]](#ref8)</sup>
+- **Postmenopausal labial fusion:** Kaplan and colleagues reported two women with complete labial-minora fusion after failed estrogen treatment who underwent blunt separation with serial Hegar dilators, followed by estrogen and massage. This is a two-patient technique report, not comparative evidence or a general nonsurgical indication.<sup>[[4]](#ref4)</sup>
+- **Calibration of a urethral meatus, catheterizable stoma, introitus, or reconstructed channel:** a metric dilator may be used in selected operative or clinic settings when its shape and diameter are appropriate. These practical uses require direct assessment and should not be inferred from the cervical device label.
+- **Neovaginal or introital assessment:** metal Hegar instruments are not interchangeable with a patient's prescribed vaginal-dilation system; tissue condition, geometry, lubrication, and the reconstructive plan determine use.
 
-### Urethral and orifice calibration
+The prior page listed Hegar calibration for recurrent UTI, routine pediatric urethroplasty, and broad gender-affirming follow-up as if they were established indications. Those are now framed as selected technique contexts pending procedure-specific source review.
 
-- **Calibration of the female urethra** — the metric Hegar set is convenient for sizing the introital and meatal caliber during workup of recurrent UTI, urethral stricture / stenosis, and post-radiation atrophy. Most operative urethral work in the male still uses [Van Buren](/docs/foundations/tools/instruments/sounds-bougies/van-buren) French-sized sounds.
-- **Pediatric urethral calibration** before pediatric hypospadias / urethroplasty, where the millimeter scale aligns with how pediatric urethras are reported.
-- **Catheterizable channel (Mitrofanoff / Monti) calibration** at the stoma in clinic — the blunt tip and 1 mm increments fit the small caliber.
-- **Vaginal-introital calibration after gender-affirming vaginoplasty** — Hegar dilators are sometimes used clinically as an in-office calibration set alongside the patient's prescribed neovaginal dilator regimen.
+Historical nonpregnant Hegar-passage or “cervical compliance” tests have appeared in cervical-insufficiency literature, but they are not a validated contemporary diagnostic use of the dilator and should not replace pregnancy-history and imaging-based assessment.<sup>[[6]](#ref6)</sup>
 
-### Diagnostic — cervical-incompetence assessment
+## Comparative Evidence Boundary
 
-- **Nonpregnant Hegar passage as a cervical-compliance test** has been used historically; **ACOG explicitly states that this test has not been validated and should not be used to diagnose cervical insufficiency**.<sup>[[4]](#ref4)[[5]](#ref5)[[6]](#ref6)[[7]](#ref7)</sup> Mentioned here because urogyn / RU clinicians sometimes inherit referrals based on this practice.
+A 2012 multicenter study randomized 120 pregnant women undergoing uterine evacuation to no dilation, Hegar dilation, or an investigational continuous controllable balloon device (40 per group). Histologic epithelial, basement-membrane, stromal injury and cervical hemorrhage were more frequent after Hegar than balloon dilation in that specific study. The results are useful device-development evidence but do not establish injury rates for all nonpregnant hysteroscopy or reconstructive uses.<sup>[[5]](#ref5)</sup>
 
-## Hegar vs Pratt vs Plastic Dilators
+Simple comparison tables claiming Hegar always requires more force, provides better tactile feedback, or is faster than Pratt or plastic dilators were not retained because those properties depend on the exact taper, step size, tissue, and technique and were not supported by a direct current-device comparison.
 
-| Feature | **Hegar** | **Pratt** | **Denniston (plastic)** |
-|---|---|---|---|
-| **Tip** | Blunt, rounded | Gradually tapered | Gradually tapered |
-| **Material** | Stainless steel (reusable) | Stainless steel (reusable) | Plastic (disposable) |
-| **Sizing** | Millimeters (1–26 mm) | French (9–79 Fr) | French equivalent |
-| **Increments** | 1 mm (or 0.5 mm) | Smaller | Similar to Pratt |
-| **Force required** | More | Less | Less |
-| **Tactile feedback at internal os** | Better — clear loss-of-resistance pop | Less distinct | Less distinct |
-| **Dilation speed** | Faster (larger steps) | Slower | Slower |
-| **North American practice** | Minority preference | Predominant for surgical abortion | Common alternative |
+## Safe-Use Principles
 
-The Society of Family Planning frames Hegar dilators as **not first-line for first-trimester surgical abortion** because of the higher axial force required, though when used (historically the half-size Hegar) the perforation rate is low (~ 0.2 / 1000 in a 10,890-patient series).<sup>[[1]](#ref1)</sup>
+1. Confirm the indication, pregnancy status, uterine/cervical orientation, and required final instrument diameter.
+2. Inspect the actual dilator for size, paired end, curvature, surface damage, and sterility/reprocessing status.
+3. Use the smallest size that enters safely, then progress gradually; do not force a stenotic or angulated canal.
+4. Use ultrasound, hysteroscopy, or another guidance strategy when anatomy or tract position is uncertain.
+5. Stop for unexpected depth, bleeding, severe pain, loss of landmarks, or concern for false passage/perforation.
+6. Apply medication, antimicrobial, and follow-up decisions from the clinical procedure—not from the Hegar name.
 
-## Technique Pearls
+## Complications
 
-- **Start small and step up sequentially** — begin at 2 mm and advance through ascending sizes, recording the **largest dilator that passes the internal os without resistance** as the cervical width.<sup>[[2]](#ref2)</sup>
-- **Cervical width is reported one size below the final dilator used** — if Hegar 4 passes without resistance but Hegar 5 requires force, the width is 4 mm.<sup>[[2]](#ref2)</sup>
-- **If resistance is met at 4 mm**, drop back to 3 mm and then 2 mm; if 2 mm still meets resistance, **record cervical width as 0 mm** and treat as a stenotic cervix.<sup>[[2]](#ref2)</sup>
-- **Stabilize the cervix with a [tenaculum](/docs/foundations/tools/instruments)** and straighten the canal along the uterine axis before passing each dilator.
-- **Apply steady, gentle axial pressure** — abrupt force is the dominant predictor of perforation and false-passage creation.<sup>[[1]](#ref1)</sup>
-- **Cervical priming before operative hysteroscopy** with **misoprostol 400 mcg vaginally ~ 12 h preop** significantly increases baseline cervical width and reduces the need for mechanical dilation — particularly valuable in nulliparous and postmenopausal urogyn patients.<sup>[[2]](#ref2)</sup>
+- Cervical laceration, uterine perforation, bleeding, pain, infection, or creation of a false passage.
+- Tissue injury from excessive force, large size jumps, or incorrect trajectory.
+- Sizing errors when millimeters and French units are mixed.
+- Trauma when a cervical model is repurposed in a urethra, stoma, or reconstructed channel without appropriate geometry and visualization.
 
-## Safety Profile
+## Historical Boundary
 
-Cervical dilation with Hegar dilators carries the following risk profile:
+The dilator is associated with German gynecologist Alfred Hegar (1830–1914). The exact provenance of every current single-ended, double-ended, curved, and half-size commercial pattern was not established here; current catalog specifications should not be treated as features of one original instrument.
 
-- **Uterine perforation** — 0.1–4 / 1000 procedures.<sup>[[1]](#ref1)</sup>
-- **Cervical laceration** — 0.1–10 / 1000 procedures; higher in adolescents.<sup>[[1]](#ref1)</sup>
-- **Cervical hemorrhage** — observed in **90%** of patients after Hegar dilation vs **32.5%** after continuous controllable balloon dilation in a randomized comparison.<sup>[[9]](#ref9)</sup>
-- **Epithelial and stromal damage** — Hegar dilation produced epithelial damage in 95%, basal-membrane damage in 82.5%, and stromal damage in 62.5% of histologic specimens, all significantly higher than balloon dilation.<sup>[[9]](#ref9)</sup>
-- **False-passage creation** — particularly when dilation is difficult; misoprostol priming reduces this risk.<sup>[[2]](#ref2)</sup>
-- **Postabortal pelvic inflammatory disease** — significantly lower with **laminaria pretreatment** vs Hegar dilation alone in a 519-patient prospective study.<sup>[[10]](#ref10)</sup>
-
-## Limitations
-
-- **Higher axial force requirement** than tapered dilators — the limiting factor in the stenotic postmenopausal cervix.
-- **Stepwise tactile cue** (loss of resistance at each new size) can be misleading in a previously instrumented cervix with a false passage.
-- **Metric-vs-French confusion** when mixed with urologic sounds on the same back table — label clearly to avoid sizing errors.
-
-## Historical Context — Alfred Hegar
-
-**Ernst Ludwig Alfred Hegar (1830–1914)** was a German gynecologist and professor at the University of Freiburg whose contributions to gynecologic instrumentation, vaginal hysterectomy technique, and the diagnostic sign of early pregnancy (Hegar's sign — softening of the lower uterine segment on bimanual exam) shaped late-19th-century gynecology. The cylindrical dilator set bearing his name remains the global standard for metric-sized cervical dilation. He is also the namesake of the [Mayo-Hegar](/docs/foundations/tools/instruments/needle-holders/mayo-hegar) and [Olsen-Hegar](/docs/foundations/tools/instruments/needle-holders/olsen-hegar) needle holders.
-
-See also: [Van Buren Sound](/docs/foundations/tools/instruments/sounds-bougies/van-buren), [Mayo-Hegar Needle Holder](/docs/foundations/tools/instruments/needle-holders/mayo-hegar), [Olsen-Hegar Needle Holder](/docs/foundations/tools/instruments/needle-holders/olsen-hegar).
+See also: [Dittel Sound](/docs/foundations/tools/instruments/sounds-bougies/dittel), [Van Buren Sound](/docs/foundations/tools/instruments/sounds-bougies/van-buren), [Mayo-Hegar Needle Holder](/docs/foundations/tools/instruments/needle-holders/mayo-hegar), and [Olsen-Hegar Needle Holder](/docs/foundations/tools/instruments/needle-holders/olsen-hegar).
 
